%%%%** Section 1.2
\subsection{Characteristics and Features}

FreeRTOS is a real time {\it kernel}.

``Operating System'' is more than a kernel.

Kernel supports
\begin{itemize}
  \item Scheduler
  \item Message passing (mailboxes)
  \item Synchronization (semaphores)
  \item Memory Management
\end{itemize}

Does not support
\begin{itemize}
  \item I/O Devices
  \item File System
  \item Networking
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide} 
Features

\begin{itemize}
  \item Source Code available (license: "modified GPL").
  \item Free for educational use and commercial use. % Commercial support packages are available and a closed source version is available.
  \item Portable to multiple micro processors.
  \item ROMable.
  \item Scalable (only builds what you need)
  \item Multitasking (up to 56 tasks)
  \item Preemptive:  Flexible number of priority levels. Multiple tasks can have same priority.
  \item Individual stacks for each task. Can
  be different sizes.
  \item Services:   queues, semphores, recursive mutexes.
  dynamic memory allocation. 
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<*>
\section{FreeRTOS History}

\href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FreeRTOS}{(source wikipedia)}
\begin{itemize}
\item Originally developed by an individual, Richard Barry, starting in 2003.
\item His company was Real Time Engineers Ltd.
\item Acquired by Amazon (AWS) in 2017.
\item License terms are now MIT license (still open source).
\end{itemize}


